# Jev API as used by this skill

Jev is TypeSafe's decision model: it answers typed questions about a piece of state with probabilities and confidence, in parallel, and generates no text. `scripts/jev_judge.py` implements everything below; this document is for troubleshooting, not for hand-rolling requests.

## Remote judging consent (read first)

A live run sends record text off the machine: `quality` sends each record and its wave's questions, `batch` sends each request line. Sending it to TypeSafe is the user's decision, so the script fails closed:

- the sc-agent client resolves credentials in `.env`, process environment, then the documented platform credential store, and
- the repository's committed `.sc/judge-jev/settings.yaml` must set `allow_remote_judge: true`. `false` is the user's no; an absent resolved credential means the live call is refused. Both are refused.

The answer is recorded once per repository, never per session: ask the user, then `python3 scripts/judge_jev_settings.py set allow_remote_judge true|false` (or the installer's `--allow-remote-judge` / `--no-remote-judge`), and commit the file. Only the user decides it: agents never write it and never infer it from existing run files or past runs. The setting is found from `--repository-root` (default `.`); reading a present settings file needs PyYAML (`DEPENDENCY.PYYAML`, exit 2, otherwise), and nothing else in `jev_judge.py` does. `--dry-run` writes request previews after normal presentation redaction and `totals {requests, bytes}` to the run directory. Previews may differ from canonical request bodies when text is redacted. `--answers-file` runs the gating with fixture answers, offline. The key is never printed, logged, written to a run file, or included in an error.

## Endpoint and auth

- `POST https://api.typesafe.ai/v1/systemone`
- sc-agent constructs and sends the authenticated request; judge-jev does not read credentials or build HTTP headers.

## Request

```json
{
  "model": "jev",
  "state": {
    "anchor": {"id": "ANC-012", "statement": "...", "scope": "..."},
    "item": {"id": "ex-42", "title": "...", "text": "..."}
  },
  "questions": {
    "relevant":     {"type": "choice", "instructions": "...", "criteria": {"yes": "...", "no": "...", "unclear": "..."}},
    "addressed":    {"type": "choice", "instructions": "...", "criteria": {"yes": "...", "no": "...", "unclear": "..."}},
    "contradicted": {"type": "choice", "instructions": "...", "criteria": {"yes": "...", "no": "...", "unclear": "..."}}
  }
}
```

- `state` may be a string, object, or array; questions reference fields with backticked paths such as `item.text`.
- `questions` is keyed by your own IDs; the `instructions` are sent, the ID is not.
- Types: `choice` (criteria map option to description; up to 255 options), `score` (2 to 10 ordered level descriptions), `noul` (probability of yes, no confidence). This skill uses `choice` so every answer carries a confidence.

## Response

```json
{
  "model": "jev-1.13.0",
  "answers": {
    "relevant":     {"type": "choice", "choice": "yes", "probabilities": {"yes": 0.96, "no": 0.03, "unclear": 0.01}, "confidence": 0.94},
    "addressed":    {"type": "choice", "choice": "no",  "probabilities": {"yes": 0.08, "no": 0.88, "unclear": 0.04}, "confidence": 0.85},
    "contradicted": {"type": "choice", "choice": "no",  "probabilities": {"yes": 0.30, "no": 0.62, "unclear": 0.08}, "confidence": 0.55}
  },
  "usage": {"input_tokens": 410, "output_tokens": 9}
}
```

`probabilities` says which option; `confidence` says whether to trust the call. sc-agent validates replies against their requests and supplies typed answers; judge-jev reads their fields for verdicts. Missing or extra answer IDs, mismatched answer types, and non-finite or out-of-range probabilities/confidence are typed response errors from the wheel. Request retries belong to sc-agent; judge-jev records its observed sends. The run file stores the full answers so thresholds can be re-tuned without re-judging.

## Models

The model selector comes from `--model` or the sc-agent configuration. Session rows record the model that answered.

## Error codes

| Code | Emitted by | Meaning and action |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `AUTH.CREDENTIALS` | sc-agent | Credential missing, malformed, or rejected. Authentication failure cancels remaining batch requests. |
| `POLICY.REMOTE_JUDGE_DISABLED` | pre-flight | `allow_remote_judge` is `false` or absent. Absent: ask the user once, record it with `judge_jev_settings.py set`, commit. `false`: only the user changes it. `--dry-run` works either way |
| `DEPENDENCY.PYYAML`, `SETTINGS.*`, `REPO.NOT_FOUND` | pre-flight | The settings file could not be read; fix what the error names. Never a silent default |
| `DECIDR.RATE_LIMITED`, `DECIDR.OVERLOADED`, `DECIDR.NETWORK` | sc-agent | Typed transport errors. See `attempts` for sends made; judge-jev does not resend requests. |
| `DECIDR.RESPONSE_INVALID` | shared response validation | The wheel rejected a provider reply or the shared client rejected a malformed controlled fixture. judge-jev adds no reply validator or resend loop. |
| `DECIDR.CONTEXT_EXCEEDED` | `decision.context_exceeded` | The provider rejected the request for context size; shorten the evidence. |
| `DECIDR.VALIDATION` | typed request validation | Request rejected; usually a malformed override |
| `VALIDATION.ANCHORS`, `VALIDATION.INPUT` | record / arguments | Fix `anchors.json` (`validate`) or the flags |
| `QUESTIONS.ROLES` | question set | An override renamed a question ID; keep the default IDs |
| `INPUT.MISSING`, `INPUT.MALFORMED`, `INPUT.EMPTY` | files | A path is not a file, not JSON, or the item text is empty |
| `FIXTURE.MISSING`, `FIXTURE.NONE` | `--answers-file` | No fixture answer for an anchor; no anchor has fixtures |
| `SCRIPT.INTERNAL` | anywhere | Unexpected exception, reported as JSON with exit 2 |

## Exit codes

A tool failure is the only nonzero exit. Exit 0 means the command ran (`success: true`); read the outcome from `data`, never from the exit code. Exit 2 means it could not do its job (`success: false` with a typed `error`). The only other exit code is 130, an interrupt.

| Subcommand | Outcome field (exit 0) |
| --- | --- |
| `batch` | No envelope: stdout is the result lines. Exit 0 when every line has an answer; exit 2 with the error envelope on stderr when any line has `ok: false` |

Exit 2 covers bad arguments, missing or unreadable files, invalid settings, `POLICY.REMOTE_JUDGE_DISABLED`, credential failure, any Jev call left without an answer, a missing dependency, and `SCRIPT.INTERNAL`. Exit 130 (`CANCELED`, `recoverable: true`) is an interrupt.

## Batch runner

Two layers and a logged stream. Nothing in them writes to the tracker: reading the results, choosing a candidate and sealing it stay with the anchor-tuning agent.

| Subcommand | Input | Output |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `batch` (low layer, tracker-neutral) | JSONL request lines on stdin or `--input <file>` | One `jev-batch-result/2` JSONL line per job, in input order, with request, run and session IDs, verdict and error fields |

Each result line is logged as a `result` event through the command Run. The event references the sc-agent session row, which holds the request and response. The wheel resolves workers and retries; authentication errors stop remaining requests. TypeSafe sends require `allow_remote_judge: true`. Dry runs send and log nothing. An answers file maps each key to one answer object or an `{"__error__": "<code>"}` object.

## Record quality

`quality (--req|--adr|--nfr <id>)... --index <raptor index> [--questions <file>] [--dry-run]` judges whether each named record is well written (REQ-JEV-0101, REQ-JEV-0209); the record flags repeat and mix, and one invocation judges every named record (REQs, then ADRs, then NFRs, each in the order given) under one run name. The records come from a Raptor import index (the JSON Raptor's `scripts/extract.py --output` writes); every record is loaded before the first call. A record with import diagnostics is refused with `RECORD.DIAGNOSTICS` and never sent, an unknown id is `RECORD.NOT_FOUND`. The decision tree is `assets/questions/quality-<kind>.json`, or the `--questions` file (for every record; a tree whose `kind` differs from the record's kind is refused with `QUALITY.TREE` before any call): a node is a wave or a leaf (`verdict`). A wave has `questions` and exactly one of `next`, an ordered list of `{when: {question id: choice}, goto: node}` (every pair matches; the first matching rule wins), or `verdict_rules`, an ordered list of `{any: {question id: choice}, verdict}` (matches when any listed question got its listed choice, or always without `any`; the first match is the verdict and the walk ends at that wave). The shipped trees hold wave 1 only, with `verdict_rules` `no` if any question answers `no`, else `unclear` if any answers `unclear`, else `yes`. Each wave is one `batch` request without an anchor, carrying only the record (as Raptor's JSON) and that wave's questions. The whole tree is checked when it loads, before any call and in `--dry-run` alike: every node, every question (a `choice` question with non-empty `instructions` and 2 to 255 `criteria`, option and description both non-empty text), every rule (each listed choice an option of its question, each `goto` a node), a last rule without conditions in each wave (so every combination of answers has an outcome), no cycle through `next`, and every later wave's statements for the record. Any failure is `QUALITY.TREE` naming the wave, question or rule; a leaf holds only its `verdict`. A JSON input holding NaN, Infinity, an integer over 4300 digits, nesting too deep to parse or a lone surrogate escape is `INPUT.MALFORMED`. No input crashes the script. Output: `data.results`, one per record, each with `record`, `tree`, `verdict`, `path` (the node ids walked) and `waves`; each wave has `node`, `request_id`, `reason` (every question in tree order: `id`, `text`, `choice`, `confidence`), `next` and `verdict` (null unless the wave ended the walk); `data.log` names the run. Every result and log event carries `tree_id` and `tree_sha256` (the file's bytes). Each wave produces result and wave events referencing session IDs, followed by a verdict event, under `<JUDGE_JEV_LOG_ROOT|~/.sc-agent>/logs/judge-jev.log.jsonl`. A wave without an answer stops the run (exit 2) with the path so far and the completed `results`. `--dry-run` reports, per record, the tree, the record and its source, and the composed wave-1 request with its size in bytes, and calls nothing; `--answers-file` keys are `<record>/<node>/r1`, `<record>/<node>`, `<node>` or `*`.

## Definitions, `compare-definition` and `doctor`

Definitions (how they reach a request, their errors, `compare-definition`, doctor's definition warnings): [definitions.md](definitions.md).

`doctor [<path>...] [--index <file>] [--repository-root <dir>]` checks the shipped trees, every tree and definitions file under `.sc/judge-jev/` and the argument paths, and calls nothing (no Jev, no key, no log, no file written). Tree errors, exit 2 with the report in `data`: a schema or model failure, a SHACL violation (edges, cycles, closed shapes), `QUESTIONS.DUPLICATE_IRI` (a question IRI declared in more than one tree file, naming every file) and `QUESTIONS.DUPLICATE_ID` (a `jj:id` used twice within one tree). Notes: `DOCTOR.INDEX_SKIPPED` when no index is given, `DOCTOR.SYMBOL_UNCHECKED` for a `path::Symbol` location.

## Input models and `validate`

Every JSON input is parsed by `strict_loads`, then into a strict pydantic v2 model (the quality tree, the question set, the answers-file envelope, the Raptor index, the batch request line). Unknown fields, wrong types and every cross-field rule are refused with the command's usual code (`QUALITY.TREE`, `VALIDATION.INPUT`, `QUESTIONS.ROLES`, `INPUT.MALFORMED`) and a message of the form `path: rule` (up to 5 problems), e.g. `nodes.wave-1.questions.context.criteria: Dictionary should have at least 2 items`. Raptor records stay open (extra fields are kept) but need a string `id`.

`validate (--tree|--questions|--answers|--index|--requests) <file>` (`-` reads stdin; exactly one kind) runs that model and the checks the other commands apply before their first request. `validate --answers` checks the answers-file envelope only (the check `--answers-file` makes before the first request), not the answers inside it. It never calls Jev, needs no API key and writes no log. On success `data` is `{kind, path, sha256}`; on failure the typed error carries the field path, with the same code `quality` or `batch --dry-run` would give. `--requests` also accepts `--gate`. `validate --tree` does not check the parts that depend on a record (statements, log events).

Invalid UTF-8 on stdin is `INPUT.MALFORMED`.

## Cost and latency

TypeSafe prices input tokens only (output is free) at a fraction of a cent per million tokens and quotes roughly 100 ms per request. A `quality` run is one request per wave per record, so judging 100 records costs effectively nothing; re-run after every edit. `--dry-run` reports `totals.bytes` to size a run without a key.

## Environment variables

| Variable | Default | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `TYPESAFE_API_KEY` | none | Process-environment input consumed by sc-agent after `.env` and before the platform credential store |

This is one credential input; sc-agent also reads configuration overrides and other provider credentials from the environment. Model, threshold and near-threshold confirmation are the flags above, with checked-in defaults. The installed troubleshooting reference specifies the Keychain, Credential Manager, and Secret Service fallback entries.

Codex environment policy for `TYPESAFE_API_KEY`: `installation-and-troubleshooting.md`.
